Adds a query builder API for building some SQL queries. 

The purpose of this QueryBuilder is to avoid dealing directly with the SQL concaneation in a string (dramatic code for illustration  below - there is a real case in the changes) and passing of params. 

It's harder to track a query when you have bits of the SQL everywhere and the param  assignation somewhere else, all wrapped in ifs. I tried to focus on having a way to conditionally build the If with the param assignation in the same line.

Q: Should we use this  everywhere? 
A: No, only if there is a heavy usage of string concatenation/ifs that would make the code harder to read. If we have a nice SQL query in a single string and it reads fine, no point on touching it.

Q: Will it cover - complicated query - ?
A: Maybe

```java
public Multi<Endpoint> getEndpointsByFilters(String filter1, String filter2, Boolean activeOnly) {
  String query = "SELECT e From Endpoint e";
  if (filter1 != null || filter2 != null || activeOnly != null) {
    query += " WHERE";
  }
  
  if (filter1 != null) {
    query += " filter1 = :filter1";
  }
  if (filter2 != null) {
    if (!query.endsWith("WHERE")) { 
      query += " AND"
    }
    query += " filter2 = :filter2";
  }
  if (activeOnly != null) {
    if (!query.endsWith("WHERE")) { 
      query += " AND"
    }
    query += " is_active = :activeOnly";
  }

  theQueryObject = // ... builds the mutiny query object

  if (filter1 != null) {
    theQueryObject.setParameter("filter1", filter1);
  }

  if (filter2 != null) {
    theQueryObject.setParameter("filter2", filter2);
  }

  if (activeOnly != null) {
   theQueryObject.setParameter("activeOnly", activeOnly)
  }

  // etc...
}
```

and instead use: 
```
public Multi<Endpoint> getEndpointsByFilters(String filter1, String filter2, Boolean activeOnly) {
  theQueryObject = QueryBuilder.builder(Endpoint.class)
    .where(
      WhereBuilder.builder()
       .ifAnd(filter1 != null, "filter1 = :filter1", "filter1", filter1)
       .ifAnd(filter2 != null, "filter2 = :filter2", "filter2", filter2)
       .ifAnd(activeOnly != null, "filter1 = :filter1", "filter1", activeOnly)
    )
    .build(
      session::createQuery  // session is a quarkus session
    );
   
}
```

